data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"To the average user, the internet feels like an invisible, ethereal \"cloud\" that","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" exists everywhere at once. In reality, the internet is one of the most massive, complex, and tangible feats of engineering","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" in human history. It is a physical web of cables, a logical system of addresses, and a strict set of rules","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" that allow billions of devices to speak the same language. To understand how it works, we must follow a single request—","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"like clicking a link—from the physical hardware up to the software layer.\n\n### The Physical Foundation: Cables and Light","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\nThe journey begins with the physical layer. The internet is not wireless; even when you use Wi-Fi, your","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" data is only traveling a few dozen feet through the air before it hits a physical wire. The \"backbone\" of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the internet consists of massive bundles of fiber-optic cables. These cables, some as thick as a human arm, run","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" under our streets and across the ocean floors, connecting continents.\n\nInside these fiber-optic cables, data is transmitted as","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" pulses of light. Using lasers, information is converted into binary code—a series of 1s and 0s","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"—represented by the presence or absence of light. In shorter distances, such as within your home, copper wires (Ether","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"net) might be used, where data travels as electrical signals. Regardless of the medium, the goal is the same:","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" moving bits of information from point A to point B at nearly the speed of light.\n\n### The Address Book: DNS","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" and IP\nOnce the physical path is established, the internet needs a way to know where to send the data. Every","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" device connected to the internet—your phone, a server in a data center, a smart toaster—is assigned a unique","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" identifier called an IP (Internet Protocol) address. An IP address is a string of numbers, such as `14","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"2.250.190.46`, that tells the network exactly where a device is located.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\n\nHowever, humans are terrible at remembering strings of numbers. We prefer names like `google.com`. This is where","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the Domain Name System (DNS) comes in. DNS acts as the internet’s phonebook. When you type a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" URL into your browser, your computer sends a request to a DNS server. The DNS server looks up the name, finds","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the corresponding IP address, and sends it back to your computer. Only once your computer has that numerical address can it begin","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the process of finding the destination.\n\n### The Delivery System: Packets and Routing\nData is rarely sent in one","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" giant, continuous stream. If you were downloading a high-resolution photo, sending it as one massive file would be inefficient","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"; if a single error occurred during transmission, the entire file would be lost. Instead, the internet breaks data down into","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" small, manageable chunks called \"packets.\"\n\nEach packet contains a portion of the actual data, but it also contains a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" \"header.\" This header is like the envelope of a letter: it lists the sender's IP address, the destination","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" IP address, and the packet's sequence number (e.g., \"Packet 3 of 500","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\").\n\nThese packets do not all take the same path. As they travel through the network, they encounter \"routers\"—","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"specialized computers that act as traffic controllers. A router looks at a packet's destination address and decides the most efficient","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" next \"hop\" to get it closer to its goal. Because the internet is decentralized, if one path is congested or","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a cable is cut, routers can dynamically reroute packets through different paths. This is why the internet is so resilient.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\n\n### Ensuring Integrity: TCP/IP\nBecause packets can take different routes and arrive out of order, the internet needs","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a way to ensure the data is reconstructed correctly. This is the job of the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).\n\nTCP","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is a \"connection-oriented\" protocol. Before any data is sent, your device and the destination server perform a \"","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"three-way handshake.\" Your device sends a synchronization request (SYN), the server responds with an acknowledgment (SYN-ACK","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"), and your device confirms it (ACK). Once the connection is established, TCP manages the flow. If a packet arrives","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" corrupted or goes missing, TCP detects the gap and requests that the sender retransmit that specific packet. Once all packets arrive","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", TCP reassembles them in the correct order, turning the chaotic stream of bits back into a coherent file.\n\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"### The Conversation: HTTP\nFinally, we reach the Application Layer, where the user actually interacts with the data. This","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is where HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) operates. \n\nOnce the TCP connection is secure, your browser sends an","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" HTTP request to the server. This request is a standardized text command, such as `GET /index.html`. This","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" tells the server, \"I would like to see the file named index.html.\" The server processes the request and sends","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" back an HTTP response. This response includes a status code (like `200 OK` if everything went well,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" or `404 Not Found` if the page doesn't exist) and the actual content of the website,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" usually in the form of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.\n\nYour browser then reads this code and renders it into the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" visual webpage you see on your screen. In a fraction of a second, a request has traveled through light-filled glass","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", been translated by a phonebook, chopped into pieces, routed across the globe, reassembled, and turned into a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" visual interface. This is the miracle of the internet.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

